    "case_id": "70",
    "case_summary": "A 56-year-old male presented with asymptomatic left-sided hydronephrosis during an evaluation for rheumatoid arthritis. The investigations included ultrasonography, intravenous urography, retrograde uretero-pyelography, CT imaging, renography, and tuberculosis screening. Radiological findings showed normal funneling and ready contrast drainage into the upper ureter, which were not typical of classical pelvi-ureteric junction obstruction. Isotope renography indicated a 25% split function in the left kidney with a poor response to frusemide diuresis. CT imaging revealed ill-defined fascial planes around the kidney and thickening around the renal hilum, indicative of localized inflammatory changes. No surgical intervention was performed due to the patient's asymptomatic status and significant ischemic heart disease. Two years later, the patient experienced worsening intermittent left loin pain, increased hydronephrosis, significant cortical thinning, and marked deterioration of renal function. After undergoing coronary artery bypass surgery, the patient chose to have a left nephrectomy. At a one-year follow-up, the patient remained pain-free. Macroscopic examination of the removed kidney showed hydronephrosis with areas of fibrosis near the renal pelvis and peri-renal fat. Microscopic examination revealed hypocellular interweaving thick collagen bundles with extensive hyalination and bland spindle cells within the fibrotic area. Inflammatory cells, including lymphocytes, eosinophils, and plasma cells, were also present, along with some irregular projections of fibrous tissue into the adjacent fat.\n\nImmunohistochemistry showed positivity for smooth muscle actin, vimentin and CD34 but negativity for ALK-1.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
